INSERT INTO public.documents (id, file_name, file_type, category, storage_path, drive_file_id, extracted_text, uploaded_at) VALUES (
  '4f6720ab-d7c6-4636-bf22-4c4e330403d2',
  'Unit_1_Last_Minute_Revision (2).docx',
  'docx',
  'General',
  'dropbox/1789649185340_Unit_1_Last_Minute_Revision__2_.docx',
  'id:8KGdhkSewzkAAAAAAAAABQ',
  'UNIT 1 — LAST-MINUTE REVISION

Computer Vision / Image Processing — connected memory sheet

THE ONE STORY — remember Unit 1 as one pipeline

IMAGE → PIXELS → MATRIX → SMOOTH → MORPHOLOGY → FLOOD FILL → RESIZE → CONVOLUTION → THRESHOLD

Think: understand the image → clean it → change shape/size → filter it → separate the object.

Do not try to memorize every paragraph. Remember the chain and rebuild the answer.



1. INTRODUCTION (very quick)

Computer Vision — A field of AI that interprets and processes visual data such as images and videos.

Memory: AI → Computer Vision → visual data.

Primary goal — Extract meaningful information from images or videos.

Memory: Goal = information.

Common application — Facial recognition.

Memory: Example = face.

2. OpenCV — the toolbox

OpenCV — Open Source Computer Vision Library; an open-source library for computer vision and machine learning.

Memory: OpenCV = toolbox.

Primary language — C++.

Memory: Core → C++.

Install in Python — pip install opencv-python

Memory: INSTALL → opencv-python

Python package — opencv-python

Memory: Package name = opencv-python.

Check version — cv2.__version__

Memory: VERSION → __version__

Read image — cv2.imread()

Memory: READ → imread.

Resize image — cv2.resize()

Memory: RESIZE → resize.

Adaptive threshold — cv2.adaptiveThreshold

Memory: ADAPTIVE → adaptiveThreshold.

OpenCV default color order — BGR.

Memory: OpenCV → BGR, not RGB.

3. Image representation

IMAGE → PIXELS → NUMBERS → MATRIX

Pixel = smallest unit of an image; stores color/intensity information.

Computer represents an image as numerical arrays/matrices.

RGB pixel = Red, Green, Blue values, typically 0–255.

0 = black/no intensity; 255 = maximum intensity.

Examples: (255,0,0)=red; (0,255,0)=green; (0,0,255)=blue; (255,165,0)=orange.

Grayscale = one intensity value per pixel (0–255).

Binary = two levels, commonly black/white (0/255).



Two common image data types — Raster images and vector images.

Memory: Raster = pixels; vector = shapes/paths.

After reading an image — Computer can apply resizing, cropping, filtering and other transformations.

Memory: READ → PROCESS.

4. Smoothing / Blurring — CLEAN THE IMAGE

WHY SMOOTH?

Reduce noise, sharp details and pixelation.

Main disadvantage: edges/details can be blurred or weakened.

Kernel = small matrix/window that moves over neighboring pixels.



Mean / Average

Idea — Replace a pixel by the average of neighboring pixels.

Memory: MEAN = ADD → DIVIDE.

3×3 kernel — 9 elements; equal weights are 1/9.

Memory: 3×3 = 9 → divide by 9.

Example — If values sum to 1150: 1150/9 = 127.77 ≈ 128.

Memory: SUM / 9.

Effect — Reduces noise but blurs the image/edges.

Memory: Clean but softer.

Gaussian

Idea — Weighted smoothing using a Gaussian distribution; closer/central pixels get more importance.

Memory: GAUSSIAN = weighted average.

Sigma (σ) — Controls smoothing; larger σ gives more blurring.

Memory: σ ↑ → blur ↑.

Kernel — Symmetric and usually odd-sized (3×3, 5×5); values normalized so their sum is 1.

Memory: ODD + SYMMETRIC + SUM=1.

Why normalize? — Preserves overall brightness.

Memory: SUM 1 → brightness stable.

Why odd-sized? — Provides a clear central pixel/anchor and symmetric alignment.

Memory: ODD → clear CENTER.

Median

Idea — Sort neighborhood values and replace the center with the middle value.

Memory: MEDIAN = SORT → MIDDLE.

Best for — Salt-and-pepper noise.

Memory: White/black noisy dots.

Why better for edges? — Median is less affected by extreme outliers than mean.

Memory: MEDIAN protects edges better than MEAN.

Process — Take neighborhood → sort → middle → replace center → slide.

Memory: SORT → MIDDLE.

Bilateral

Idea — Smooths noise while preserving edges using spatial distance AND intensity difference.

Memory: BILATERAL = DISTANCE + INTENSITY.

Edge preservation — Similar-intensity neighbors get more weight; large intensity differences get less.

Memory: SIMILAR → MORE; DIFFERENT → LESS.

Limitation — More computationally expensive.

Memory: Better edges, more calculation.

Box

Idea — Moving-average/mean filter; all pixels in the kernel are treated equally.

Memory: BOX = MEAN.

Advantage — Simple, fast, computationally efficient.

Memory: FAST + SIMPLE.

Limitation — Can blur edges because intensity differences are ignored.

Memory: BOX blurs edges.

FILTER MEMORY

MEAN/BOX → average all → simple smoothing → edges can blur.

GAUSSIAN → weighted average → center more important.

MEDIAN → sort + middle → salt-and-pepper → better edge preservation.

BILATERAL → distance + intensity → smooth + preserve edges → expensive.



5. Morphology — WORK WITH SHAPE

MORPHOLOGY = manipulate STRUCTURE/SHAPE

Usually applied to binary or grayscale images.

Structuring element/kernel = small matrix used to probe/modify neighborhoods.

Basic operations: EROSION and DILATION.



Erosion

Idea — Shrinks/erodes object boundaries.

Memory: EROSION = SHRINK.

Binary rule — Output is 1 only if ALL pixels under the kernel are 1; otherwise 0.

Memory: ALL must be 1.

Applications — Noise removal, boundary extraction, object separation, shape simplification.

Memory: SHRINK → REMOVE / SEPARATE.

Dilation

Idea — Expands object boundaries.

Memory: DILATION = GROW.

Binary rule — Output is 1 if AT LEAST ONE pixel under the kernel is 1.

Memory: ANY 1 is enough.

Applications — Bridge gaps, fill small holes/gaps, thicken lines and boundaries.

Memory: GROW → CONNECT / FILL.

OPENING vs CLOSING — memorize this pair

OPENING = EROSION → DILATION → removes small objects/noise; smooths protrusions.

CLOSING = DILATION → EROSION → fills small holes/gaps; connects parts.

Memory: OPENING cleans/removes. CLOSING closes holes.



Morphological Gradient — Dilation − Erosion; used for boundary/edge extraction.

Memory: GRADIENT = BORDER.

Morphology kernel — A matrix defining the neighborhood for the operation.

Memory: Kernel = neighborhood template.

6. Flood Fill — FILL A CONNECTED REGION

Idea — Starts at a seed point and fills connected pixels with similar color/intensity.

Memory: SEED → SPREAD.

Seed point — Starting point for flood fill.

Memory: SEED = START.

4-connected — North, South, East, West.

Memory: 4 = four sides.

8-connected — All 8 surrounding neighbors, including diagonals.

Memory: 8 = sides + corners.

Typical use — Fill closed/similar-colored areas; also region growing/segmentation.

Memory: Paint-bucket idea.

High tolerance — Unintended areas may also be filled.

Memory: Too high → spreads too far.

No boundary — The entire image may be filled.

Memory: No boundary → whole image.

7. Resizing + Interpolation

RESIZE = change dimensions | INTERPOLATION = estimate new pixel values

Wrong aspect ratio → image may appear distorted.

OpenCV resize function: cv2.resize().

Interpolation parameter determines the interpolation method.



Method

Memory hook

Key idea

Nearest Neighbor

1 pixel / FAST

Nearest original pixel; fast but blocky/pixelated

Bilinear

4 pixels

Weighted average of 4 neighbors; smoother, may blur

Bicubic

4×4 = 16

Cubic calculation; good for enlargement; cv2.INTER_CUBIC

Lanczos

8×8 = 64

Good detail/downscaling; may create ringing

Area

AREA = DOWNSCALE

Averages an area; designed for downscaling

INTERPOLATION LADDER

Nearest → 1 | Bilinear → 4 | Bicubic → 16 | Lanczos → 64 | Area → downscaling



8. Convolution — APPLY A KERNEL/FILTER

Idea — A kernel slides over the image; corresponding values are multiplied and added to calculate new pixel values.

Memory: SLIDE → MULTIPLY → ADD.

3×3 kernel — 9 elements.

Memory: 3×3 = 9.

Edge-detection kernel — Highlights edges.

Memory: EDGE KERNEL → EDGES.

Normalized convolution filter — Kernel values sum to 1.

Memory: SUM = 1.

Sharpening — Uses high-pass filtering.

Memory: HIGH-PASS → SHARPEN.

Padding — Adds border values so convolution can preserve image size.

Memory: PADDING → PROTECT SIZE.

DO NOT CONFUSE

Convolution = the general kernel operation.

Smoothing = one use of filtering/convolution to reduce noise/details.

Different kernels can produce blur, edge detection or sharpening.



9. Thresholding — SEPARATE OBJECT FROM BACKGROUND

Idea — Segmentation technique that assigns pixel values based on a threshold, usually on a grayscale image.

Memory: THRESHOLD = LIMIT.

Basic binary rule — Above threshold → 255; below threshold → 0.

Memory: ABOVE → WHITE; BELOW → BLACK.

Goal — Separate foreground objects from the background.

Memory: THRESHOLD = SEPARATE.

Typical output — Binary image.

Memory: THRESHOLD → BINARY.

Simple/global thresholding — Uses one global threshold value.

Memory: GLOBAL = ONE.

Adaptive thresholding — Calculates thresholds for local regions.

Memory: ADAPTIVE = LOCAL.

OpenCV adaptive function — cv2.adaptiveThreshold

Memory: ADAPTIVE → adaptiveThreshold.

Otsu''s method — The Unit 1 question sheet lists Otsu’s method as a commonly used adaptive-thresholding method.

Memory: Exam sheet wording: OTSU.

OpenCV threshold types

THRESH_BINARY → above threshold 255, otherwise 0.

THRESH_BINARY_INV → inverse of binary.

THRESH_TRUNC → above threshold becomes threshold value; others unchanged.

THRESH_TOZERO → below threshold becomes 0; others unchanged.

THRESH_TOZERO_INV → inverse of TOZERO.

10. DESCRIPTIVE ANSWER FORMULA

If you forget the exact paragraph, rebuild the answer with 3 parts

1) DEFINITION — What is it?

2) PROCESS — How does it work?

3) EFFECT / USE — What does it do or where is it used?

Example: “Erosion is a morphological operation. It uses a structuring element and shrinks objects. It is used for noise removal and object separation.”



11. MORNING 15–20 MINUTE REVISION

Do NOT reread everything

1. Say the pipeline aloud: IMAGE → SMOOTH → MORPHOLOGY → FLOOD → RESIZE → CONVOLUTION → THRESHOLD.

2. Recall filters: MEAN / GAUSSIAN / MEDIAN / BILATERAL / BOX.

3. Recall morphology: EROSION = SHRINK; DILATION = GROW; OPENING = E→D; CLOSING = D→E.

4. Recall flood fill: SEED; 4-connected; 8-connected; tolerance.

5. Recall interpolation: 1 → 4 → 16 → 64 + AREA.

6. Recall convolution: SLIDE → MULTIPLY → ADD; padding = preserve size; high-pass = sharpen.

7. Recall threshold: GLOBAL = one; ADAPTIVE = local; output = binary.

8. Recall OpenCV commands: imread, resize, adaptiveThreshold, __version__, opencv-python, BGR.

9. For any long answer: DEFINITION → PROCESS → EFFECT/USE.



12. FINAL ONE-LINE MEMORY MAP

OpenCV reads → Image is pixels/matrix → Smooth noise → Morphology changes shape → Flood Fill fills region → Resize changes size → Convolution filters → Threshold separates.

UNIT 1 — QUICK REVISION COMPLETE',
  '2026-09-17T12:46:25.932+00:00'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

