INSERT INTO public.document_chunks (id, document_id, chunk_text, chunk_index, embedding) VALUES (
  'bc1f830b-8e81-4092-ba61-c08acacd13fe',
  '4f6720ab-d7c6-4636-bf22-4c4e330403d2',
  'UNIT 1 — LAST-MINUTE REVISION Computer Vision / Image Processing — connected memory sheet THE ONE STORY — remember Unit 1 as one pipeline IMAGE → PIXELS → MATRIX → SMOOTH → MORPHOLOGY → FLOOD FILL → RESIZE → CONVOLUTION → THRESHOLD Think: understand the image → clean it → change shape/size → filter it → separate the object. Do not try to memorize every paragraph. Remember the chain and rebuild the answer. 1. INTRODUCTION (very quick) Computer Vision — A field of AI that interprets and processes visual data such as images and videos. Memory: AI → Computer Vision → visual data. Primary goal — Extract meaningful information from images or videos. Memory: Goal = information. Common application — Facial recognition. Memory: Example = face. 2. OpenCV — the toolbox OpenCV — Open Source Computer Vision Library; an open-source library for computer vision and machine learning. Memory: OpenCV = toolbox. Primary language — C++. Memory: Core → C++. Install in Python — pip install opencv-python Memory: INSTALL → opencv-python Python package — opencv-python Memory: Package name = opencv-python. Check version — cv2.__version__ Memory: VERSION → __version__ Read image — cv2.imread() Memory: READ → imread. Resize image — cv2.resize() Memory: RESIZE → resize. Adaptive threshold — cv2.adaptiveThreshold Memory: ADAPTIVE → adaptiveThreshold. OpenCV default color order — BGR. Memory: OpenCV → BGR, not RGB. 3. Image representation IMAGE → PIXELS → NUMBERS → MATRIX Pixel = smallest unit of an image; stores color/intensity information. Computer represents an image as numerical arrays/matrices. RGB pixel = Red, Green, Blue values, typically 0–255. 0 = black/no intensity; 255 = maximum intensity. Examples: (255,0,0)=red; (0,255,0)=green; (0,0,255)=blue; (255,165,0)=orange. Grayscale = one intensity value per pixel (0–255). Binary = two levels, commonly black/white (0/255). Two common image data types — Raster images and vector images. Memory: Raster = pixels; vector = shapes/paths. After reading an image — Computer can apply resizing, cropping, filtering and other transformations. Memory: READ → PROCESS. 4. Smoothing / Blurring — CLEAN THE IMAGE WHY SMOOTH? Reduce noise, sharp details and pixelation. Main disadvantage: edges/details can be blurred or weakened. Kernel = small matrix/window that moves over neighboring pixels. Mean / Average Idea — Replace a pixel by the average of neighboring pixels. Memory: MEAN = ADD → DIVIDE. 3×3 kernel — 9 elements; equal weights are 1/9. Memory: 3×3 = 9 → divide by 9. Example — If values sum to 1150:',
  0,
