INSERT INTO public.document_chunks (id, document_id, chunk_text, chunk_index, embedding) VALUES (
  '2a3a2d03-e2ce-4c13-bbec-c4bab41c83e7',
  '4f6720ab-d7c6-4636-bf22-4c4e330403d2',
  'matrix/window that moves over neighboring pixels. Mean / Average Idea — Replace a pixel by the average of neighboring pixels. Memory: MEAN = ADD → DIVIDE. 3×3 kernel — 9 elements; equal weights are 1/9. Memory: 3×3 = 9 → divide by 9. Example — If values sum to 1150: 1150/9 = 127.77 ≈ 128. Memory: SUM / 9. Effect — Reduces noise but blurs the image/edges. Memory: Clean but softer. Gaussian Idea — Weighted smoothing using a Gaussian distribution; closer/central pixels get more importance. Memory: GAUSSIAN = weighted average. Sigma (σ) — Controls smoothing; larger σ gives more blurring. Memory: σ ↑ → blur ↑. Kernel — Symmetric and usually odd-sized (3×3, 5×5); values normalized so their sum is 1. Memory: ODD + SYMMETRIC + SUM=1. Why normalize? — Preserves overall brightness. Memory: SUM 1 → brightness stable. Why odd-sized? — Provides a clear central pixel/anchor and symmetric alignment. Memory: ODD → clear CENTER. Median Idea — Sort neighborhood values and replace the center with the middle value. Memory: MEDIAN = SORT → MIDDLE. Best for — Salt-and-pepper noise. Memory: White/black noisy dots. Why better for edges? — Median is less affected by extreme outliers than mean. Memory: MEDIAN protects edges better than MEAN. Process — Take neighborhood → sort → middle → replace center → slide. Memory: SORT → MIDDLE. Bilateral Idea — Smooths noise while preserving edges using spatial distance AND intensity difference. Memory: BILATERAL = DISTANCE + INTENSITY. Edge preservation — Similar-intensity neighbors get more weight; large intensity differences get less. Memory: SIMILAR → MORE; DIFFERENT → LESS. Limitation — More computationally expensive. Memory: Better edges, more calculation. Box Idea — Moving-average/mean filter; all pixels in the kernel are treated equally. Memory: BOX = MEAN. Advantage — Simple, fast, computationally efficient. Memory: FAST + SIMPLE. Limitation — Can blur edges because intensity differences are ignored. Memory: BOX blurs edges. FILTER MEMORY MEAN/BOX → average all → simple smoothing → edges can blur. GAUSSIAN → weighted average → center more important. MEDIAN → sort + middle → salt-and-pepper → better edge preservation. BILATERAL → distance + intensity → smooth + preserve edges → expensive. 5. Morphology — WORK WITH SHAPE MORPHOLOGY = manipulate STRUCTURE/SHAPE Usually applied to binary or grayscale images. Structuring element/kernel = small matrix used to probe/modify neighborhoods. Basic operations: EROSION and DILATION. Erosion Idea — Shrinks/erodes object boundaries. Memory: EROSION = SHRINK. Binary rule',
