INSERT INTO public.document_chunks (id, document_id, chunk_text, chunk_index, embedding) VALUES (
  '07c20a5f-244f-4b97-a760-f086c60d0aa1',
  '4f6720ab-d7c6-4636-bf22-4c4e330403d2',
  '→ smooth + preserve edges → expensive. 5. Morphology — WORK WITH SHAPE MORPHOLOGY = manipulate STRUCTURE/SHAPE Usually applied to binary or grayscale images. Structuring element/kernel = small matrix used to probe/modify neighborhoods. Basic operations: EROSION and DILATION. Erosion Idea — Shrinks/erodes object boundaries. Memory: EROSION = SHRINK. Binary rule — Output is 1 only if ALL pixels under the kernel are 1; otherwise 0. Memory: ALL must be 1. Applications — Noise removal, boundary extraction, object separation, shape simplification. Memory: SHRINK → REMOVE / SEPARATE. Dilation Idea — Expands object boundaries. Memory: DILATION = GROW. Binary rule — Output is 1 if AT LEAST ONE pixel under the kernel is 1. Memory: ANY 1 is enough. Applications — Bridge gaps, fill small holes/gaps, thicken lines and boundaries. Memory: GROW → CONNECT / FILL. OPENING vs CLOSING — memorize this pair OPENING = EROSION → DILATION → removes small objects/noise; smooths protrusions. CLOSING = DILATION → EROSION → fills small holes/gaps; connects parts. Memory: OPENING cleans/removes. CLOSING closes holes. Morphological Gradient — Dilation − Erosion; used for boundary/edge extraction. Memory: GRADIENT = BORDER. Morphology kernel — A matrix defining the neighborhood for the operation. Memory: Kernel = neighborhood template. 6. Flood Fill — FILL A CONNECTED REGION Idea — Starts at a seed point and fills connected pixels with similar color/intensity. Memory: SEED → SPREAD. Seed point — Starting point for flood fill. Memory: SEED = START. 4-connected — North, South, East, West. Memory: 4 = four sides. 8-connected — All 8 surrounding neighbors, including diagonals. Memory: 8 = sides + corners. Typical use — Fill closed/similar-colored areas; also region growing/segmentation. Memory: Paint-bucket idea. High tolerance — Unintended areas may also be filled. Memory: Too high → spreads too far. No boundary — The entire image may be filled. Memory: No boundary → whole image. 7. Resizing + Interpolation RESIZE = change dimensions | INTERPOLATION = estimate new pixel values Wrong aspect ratio → image may appear distorted. OpenCV resize function: cv2.resize(). Interpolation parameter determines the interpolation method. Method Memory hook Key idea Nearest Neighbor 1 pixel / FAST Nearest original pixel; fast but blocky/pixelated Bilinear 4 pixels Weighted average of 4 neighbors; smoother, may blur Bicubic 4×4 = 16 Cubic calculation; good for enlargement; cv2.INTER_CUBIC Lanczos 8×8 = 64 Good detail/downscaling; may create ringing Area AREA = DOWNSCALE Averages an area; designed for downscaling INTERPOLATION LADDER Nearest → 1',
  2,
