INSERT INTO public.document_chunks (id, document_id, chunk_text, chunk_index, embedding) VALUES (
  '7814ba48-8bde-4ec6-bafe-62a9913e3567',
  '4f6720ab-d7c6-4636-bf22-4c4e330403d2',
  'original pixel; fast but blocky/pixelated Bilinear 4 pixels Weighted average of 4 neighbors; smoother, may blur Bicubic 4×4 = 16 Cubic calculation; good for enlargement; cv2.INTER_CUBIC Lanczos 8×8 = 64 Good detail/downscaling; may create ringing Area AREA = DOWNSCALE Averages an area; designed for downscaling INTERPOLATION LADDER Nearest → 1 | Bilinear → 4 | Bicubic → 16 | Lanczos → 64 | Area → downscaling 8. Convolution — APPLY A KERNEL/FILTER Idea — A kernel slides over the image; corresponding values are multiplied and added to calculate new pixel values. Memory: SLIDE → MULTIPLY → ADD. 3×3 kernel — 9 elements. Memory: 3×3 = 9. Edge-detection kernel — Highlights edges. Memory: EDGE KERNEL → EDGES. Normalized convolution filter — Kernel values sum to 1. Memory: SUM = 1. Sharpening — Uses high-pass filtering. Memory: HIGH-PASS → SHARPEN. Padding — Adds border values so convolution can preserve image size. Memory: PADDING → PROTECT SIZE. DO NOT CONFUSE Convolution = the general kernel operation. Smoothing = one use of filtering/convolution to reduce noise/details. Different kernels can produce blur, edge detection or sharpening. 9. Thresholding — SEPARATE OBJECT FROM BACKGROUND Idea — Segmentation technique that assigns pixel values based on a threshold, usually on a grayscale image. Memory: THRESHOLD = LIMIT. Basic binary rule — Above threshold → 255; below threshold → 0. Memory: ABOVE → WHITE; BELOW → BLACK. Goal — Separate foreground objects from the background. Memory: THRESHOLD = SEPARATE. Typical output — Binary image. Memory: THRESHOLD → BINARY. Simple/global thresholding — Uses one global threshold value. Memory: GLOBAL = ONE. Adaptive thresholding — Calculates thresholds for local regions. Memory: ADAPTIVE = LOCAL. OpenCV adaptive function — cv2.adaptiveThreshold Memory: ADAPTIVE → adaptiveThreshold. Otsu''s method — The Unit 1 question sheet lists Otsu’s method as a commonly used adaptive-thresholding method. Memory: Exam sheet wording: OTSU. OpenCV threshold types THRESH_BINARY → above threshold 255, otherwise 0. THRESH_BINARY_INV → inverse of binary. THRESH_TRUNC → above threshold becomes threshold value; others unchanged. THRESH_TOZERO → below threshold becomes 0; others unchanged. THRESH_TOZERO_INV → inverse of TOZERO. 10. DESCRIPTIVE ANSWER FORMULA If you forget the exact paragraph, rebuild the answer with 3 parts 1) DEFINITION — What is it? 2) PROCESS — How does it work? 3) EFFECT / USE — What does it do or where is it used? Example: “Erosion is a morphological operation. It uses a structuring element and shrinks objects. It is',
